# Node
node_modules
npm-debug.log

# package-lock.json is deliberately NOT ignored. It used to be, which meant the
# Dockerfile could never see it: "COPY package.json package-lock.json* ./"
# matched zero files for the lock, the trailing glob made that a non-error, and
# the image fell back to an unpinned "npm install" that re-resolved every "^"
# range against the registry at build time. CI tested one dependency tree and
# production shipped another, with no signal that they differed. The lock is
# force-added to git (listed in .gitignore but tracked), so it is always in the
# build context; the Dockerfile now COPYs it without a glob, so if anyone
# re-adds it here the build fails loudly instead of silently going unpinned.

# Next.js build output
.next
.next/cache
out
dist

# Installer artifacts (a ~94 MB APK and a ~16 MB zip, both git-tracked).
# In production these are BIND-MOUNTED READ-ONLY into the container from
# /opt/restaurant-dash/public/downloads, deliberately not baked into the image,
# so baking them would push ~110 MB to the registry on every deploy only for the
# mount to shadow it. Refreshing them is a separate, deliberate scp — shipping a
# 94 MB APK to every restaurant is a release decision, not a side effect of a
# dashboard code push.
#
# Consequence for LOCAL builds: an image built without that bind mount serves
# 404 for /downloads/*. rd-deploy asserts the mount is present before and after
# every dashboard deploy so this cannot regress unnoticed in production.
public/downloads

# Local dev artifacts
.env
.env.local
.env.*
.vercel

